# iFive mini 4S — Android 16 GSI release {{DATE}}

Partition map: GSI layout (system 3072 MiB @ 296 MiB, metadata 16 MiB @ 3368 MiB, userdata @ 3448 MiB).
Super image: Android 16 GSI (TrebleDroid a64_bvS) + Tinker Board vendor partition with the patched HWC (HWC1 mix-policy + fence fix, sha 83101dff) and the charging policy service. Kernel: 4.19 release config.

## Contents
- `images/resource.img` (RSCE, GSI blkdevparts), `images/kernel.img` (KRNL), `images/ramdisk.img` (KRNL)
- `images/super.img` (liblp, system/vendor/odm/product), `images/metadata.img`, `images/userdata-head-zero.img`
- `loader/RK3288UbootLoader_V2.30.10.bin`
- `devices/<board>/` — the per-board rescue data (see "Restoring a wiped tablet")
- `flash.sh` (whole-image flash), `restore.sh` (wiped-tablet restore), `write-loader-head.sh` (loader-area restore), `manifest.json` (the write map derived at build time), `tools/rkdeveloptool`, `SHA256SUMS` (every images/*.img, loader/*.bin and devices/* file), `BUILD-INFO.txt`

This package holds the GSI image set and the Rockchip parameter structures of the supported boards only. It contains **no stock Android 11 partition images** (no system, boot, recovery, vendor from the original firmware). The original stock backup stays on the owner's machine; the copy in this package is the part that is needed to make a wiped tablet bootable again.

Both `flash.sh` and `restore.sh` take their write addresses from `manifest.json`, which is derived at build time from the partition table of the device tree inside `images/resource.img` and from the parameter structure of each board. Neither script carries an address of its own, so the two cannot disagree about where an image goes.

## Flashing
Linux x86_64 with libusb-1.0; rkdeveloptool is bundled (set RKDEVELOPTOOL to use another). Erases system and all user data, unless `--keep-userdata` is given.
1. Put the tablet in Rockchip download mode, so it enumerates as USB `2207:320a`. From a running system `adb reboot loader` does it; check with `./tools/rkdeveloptool ld`, which prints `Loader` or `Maskrom`. `flash.sh` accepts either state (the Maskrom steps are in "A tablet with a blank eMMC").
2. `./flash.sh --dry-run` (no device touched), then `./flash.sh --yes`.
   `./flash.sh --keep-userdata` leaves `/data` alone: it skips the two writes that destroy it (the `metadata` partition and the first 1 MiB of `userdata`, which holds the ext4 superblock) and still replaces system, vendor, odm, kernel, ramdisk and the device tree. `/data` on this tablet is a plain ext4 with no encryption behind it (`ro.crypto.state=unsupported`, `/dev/block/mmcblk0p13` mounted directly), so it stays readable when those two writes are skipped. Measured here: after a `--keep-userdata` flash of a package whose `build.prop` identity keys had changed, the oldest app data directories in `/data/data` still dated from the boot before the flash (10 hours earlier) and all 8 user-installed apps were still installed - Android did not factory reset itself. Still, back up anything you cannot lose: that is one tablet, one boot.
3. Script verifies SHA-256, writes each image, reads back and compares, then resets. First boot takes several minutes.

## Restoring a wiped tablet

`restore.sh` brings a tablet with an erased partition table back to a bootable state. It works for every board in `devices/`, not only for the one this release was built for.

**The damage class this section covers: the bootloader is intact, the parameter structures are erased.** The tablet still has its Rockchip bootloader on the eMMC, so it enumerates in Loader mode and every region the restore writes is reachable with `rkdeveloptool`. A tablet whose bootloader is gone as well (a blank eMMC) needs the extra steps of "A tablet with a blank eMMC" below — `restore.sh` alone does not cover it.

### Picking a board

The package holds one directory per supported board:

```
devices/ifive-mini4s/
  device.json           board id, display name, the parameter LBAs, the loader-area note
  parameter-p1.img      that board's p1 parameter partition (4 MiB, 4 PARM structures)
  parameter-loader.img  that board's loader-area parameter region (4 MiB, 8 PARM structures)
  head-4M.bin           the board's first 4 MiB of eMMC (idblock + u-boot.itb)
  head-8M.bin           the board's first 8 MiB of eMMC
```

`restore.sh` without `--board` detects the board itself: it reads the parameter structure the tablet holds at tool LBA 0x2000 and compares it against the `parameter-p1.img` of every board. It prints what it matched:

```
== detected board ifive-mini4s
```

If the tablet's parameter structure matches no board, the script refuses to write and lists the boards it knows:

```
STOP: the device matches no known board (ifive-mini4s); pass --board
```

Use `--board <id>` to pick a board by hand.

### Where the write addresses come from

Nothing in `restore.sh` carries an address. Every address is derived from the data inside the package:

- The **device tree** inside `images/resource.img` holds the partition table in the kernel command line (`blkdevparts=mmcblk0:16M@16M(resource),...`). Its offsets are in MiB.
- The **parameter structure** of the board holds the table the bootloader uses (`mtdparts=rk29xxnand:0x00008000@0x00006000(resource),...`). Its offsets are in sectors and are already tool LBAs.

Both tables must describe the same disk, and the script refuses to write when they do not. The rule it applies:

- `system` and every partition **before** it: the two tables must agree exactly.
- `system` itself: the device tree may give it more room. The difference `delta` is computed from the two tables (system size in the device tree minus system size in the parameter), never written down as a constant.
- every partition **behind** `system`: its device tree address must be exactly `delta` sectors further out than the parameter says.

For this GSI the difference is 1536 MiB (0x300000 sectors): the device tree gives `system` 3072 MiB, the stock parameter gives it 1536 MiB. The GSI super image of 1650458624 B (1574 MiB) does not fit the stock 1536 MiB system partition, so the GSI doubles it and everything behind it moves by the same amount.

Any other difference between the two tables is fatal: the script names the partition that disagrees and stops without writing.

### How the eMMC is addressed

The eMMC of these tablets starts with a 4 MiB loader area that holds the bootloader itself:

- eMMC sector 0x40: the idblock (Rockchip boot hash / signature area)
- eMMC sector 0x44: `u-boot.itb`, tagged `RK32` in its first bytes
- eMMC sectors 0x2000..0x3FFF: the loader-area parameter region (8 copies of the parameter structure, 512 KiB apart)
- eMMC sectors 0x4000..0x5FFF: the p1 "uboot" partition (4 copies of the parameter structure, 1 MiB apart)

`rkdeveloptool` does not number sectors from 0. Its sector 0 is eMMC sector 0x2000, so:

```
tool LBA = eMMC sector - 0x2000
```

That is also the mtdparts offset of the partition table, so the tool LBA of a partition is the same number the parameter file lists. The whole loader area (eMMC sectors 0x0000..0x1FFF) lies **below** tool LBA 0 and is therefore not reachable by any `rkdeveloptool` LBA — it can neither be read nor written that way.

### The two kinds of damage

**1. The parameter structures are erased** (partition table unusable, the tablet cannot boot at all).
This is the common case after a partial erase, and it is the damage class `restore.sh` covers on its own: the bootloader is still on the eMMC, so the tablet enumerates in Loader mode. `restore.sh` writes both parameter structures back first and then the whole GSI image set. Everything in that path is reachable with `rkdeveloptool`, so Linux alone is enough.

**2. The loader area is erased** (idblock and `u-boot.itb` gone, so not even the parameter structures can be found).
This area is below tool LBA 0 and `rkdeveloptool` cannot write it. The fix is a different tool: write `devices/<board>/head-4M.bin` at **eMMC sector 0** — with `./write-loader-head.sh --board <id>` from this package, or with RKDevTool or AFPTool on Windows (or any other tool that can write the eMMC from sector 0) — and then run `restore.sh` as in case 1. See "A tablet with a blank eMMC" below for the full order.

`head-4M.bin` is the exact first 4 MiB of that board's eMMC (idblock at sector 0x40, `u-boot.itb` at sector 0x44), taken from the owner's stock backup. `head-8M.bin` is the whole 8 MiB dump (sectors 0x0000..0x3FFF), i.e. the loader area plus the loader-area parameter region; it is included so the owner can inspect or re-derive the pieces.

### What `restore.sh` writes

For the board of this release the derived write map is (the same numbers are in `manifest.json`, and for any other board they are derived the same way):

|what|tool LBA|raw sector|MiB|
|---|---|---|---|
|`devices/ifive-mini4s/parameter-loader.img` (loader-area parameter region)|0x0000|0x02000|4|
|`devices/ifive-mini4s/parameter-p1.img` (p1 "uboot" partition)|0x2000|0x04000|8|
|`images/resource.img`|0x6000|0x08000|16|
|`images/kernel.img`|0xE000|0x10000|32|
|`images/ramdisk.img`|0x16000|0x18000|48|
|`images/super.img`|0x92000|0x94000|296|
|`images/metadata.img`|0x692000|0x694000|3368|
|`images/userdata-head-zero.img`|0x6BA000|0x6BC000|3448|

### The commands

```sh
./restore.sh                 # detect the board, restore everything
./restore.sh --board ifive-mini4s
./restore.sh --parameters-only   # write only the two parameter structures
./restore.sh --plan          # print the derived write map, touch no device
./restore.sh --maskrom       # the tablet is in Maskrom mode: push the loader into RAM first
```

`--parameters-only` writes the loader-area parameter copy (tool LBA 0x0000) and the p1 parameter partition (tool LBA 0x2000) and nothing else: no image partition is touched. Use it when the tablet already runs the GSI and only its parameter structures are missing.

It is not the same as a full restore. A full restore also writes `images/kernel.img` to 0xE000, which replaces the kernel the tablet has with the one in this package. On the tablet this release was built for the kernel that runs is a different build: its `KRNL` container is 0x8b9510 bytes against 0x8b9578 bytes for the kernel in this package, a difference of 104 bytes. `--parameters-only` leaves the running kernel alone.

The script checks the images against `SHA256SUMS`, refuses to start unless the tablet is enumerated (`rkdeveloptool ld`), shows the derived write map, and then asks for the word `restore` before it writes anything:

```
Type restore to continue:
```

Every write is read back and compared; a mismatch stops the script. `./restore.sh --yes` skips the confirmation word (it still does all checks and still verifies every write). It does not reset the tablet at the end; run `tools/rkdeveloptool rd` (or reboot it by hand) when it says `RESTORE COMPLETE`.

If the loader area was erased as well (case 2), do this first on Windows:

1. Put the tablet in Maskrom mode.
2. RKDevTool (or AFPTool): write `devices/<board>/head-4M.bin` at address **0** (eMMC sector 0, 4 MiB).
3. Then run `./restore.sh` on Linux as above.

### What this package does not contain

No stock (Android 11) partition images — no stock `system`, `boot`, `recovery` or `vendor`. The tablet is restored with the Android 16 GSI image set, not with the original firmware. The full stock backup remains on the owner's machine; only the loader head and the two parameter structures were copied into this package.

### Adding another board

A board entry is one directory under `devices/` plus one JSON file that describes it. To add a board:

1. **Take a stock backup of that board.** At least two files are needed:
   - the **p1 parameter partition** (4 MiB) — the parameter structure of the "uboot" partition. `parameter-p1.img` is this image.
   - the **first 8 MiB of the eMMC** — for `head-8M.bin` and its first 4 MiB for `head-4M.bin`. The loader-area parameter copy (`parameter-loader.img`) is the 4 MiB at byte offset 0x2000..0x3FFF of that dump: 8 copies of the parameter structure, 512 KiB apart.

2. **Put the files in `devices/<board>/`:**

   ```
   devices/<board>/device.json
   devices/<board>/parameter-p1.img
   devices/<board>/parameter-loader.img    (optional)
   devices/<board>/head-4M.bin             (optional)
   devices/<board>/head-8M.bin             (optional)
   ```

3. **Write `device.json`:**

   |field|meaning|
   |---|---|
   |`board`|the board id, the name of the directory and the value `--board` takes|
   |`display_name`|the name shown when the board is detected|
   |`stock_backup_dir`|where the stock backup of that board lives (build time only)|
   |`p1_parameter_gz`|the file name of the p1 parameter image inside that backup|
   |`bootloader_8m_gz`|the file name of the 8 MiB eMMC dump inside that backup|
   |`parameter_region_lba`|the tool LBA of the loader-area parameter copy|
   |`p1_lba`|the tool LBA of the p1 parameter partition|
   |`loader_area_note`|a note about the loader area, copied into the package|
   |`head4_sha256`, `head8_sha256`, `p1_sha256`, `parameter_loader_sha256`|the digests the build verifies against|

4. **Two constraints the new board must satisfy**, or `restore.sh` refuses to write and the guard fails the build:

   - **`parameter-p1.img` must be exactly 4 MiB and hold exactly 4 PARM structures** (at offsets 0, 0x100000, 0x200000, 0x300000). The guard checks the size and the count.
   - **The two partition tables must satisfy the same `system`-anchor rule** as described above: the device tree of the package and the parameter structure of the new board must agree on every partition up to and including `system`, and every partition behind `system` must sit exactly `delta` sectors further out, where `delta` is the difference of the two `system` sizes. If the two tables disagree about any other partition, `restore.sh` names that partition and stops before writing.

   The board's `p1_lba` must also match where the parameter structure itself puts its "uboot" partition, and the `RK32` tag must be present at byte offset 0x8800 of `head-4M.bin`.


## A tablet with a blank eMMC

**What "blank" means here:** no bootloader, no parameter structures, no images — the eMMC holds nothing of the original firmware, not even the Rockchip bootloader. Such a tablet cannot boot at all, and its boot ROM finds no bootloader on the eMMC, so it enumerates on USB as **2207:320a in Maskrom mode** (the same USB id as Loader mode; only `rkdeveloptool ld` tells the two apart — it prints `Maskrom` or `Loader`).

### The order of the recovery

1. **Maskrom → Loader mode.** While the tablet is in Maskrom mode not one eMMC sector can be read or written: the Rockchip loader has to run from RAM first.
   - `./restore.sh --maskrom` does that: it pushes the bundled loader (`loader/RK3288UbootLoader_V2.30.10.bin`) into the tablet's RAM with `rkdeveloptool db` and continues with the normal flow. It is off by default, and it has failed on this tablet (see below).
   - The documented route is RKDevTool/AFPTool on Windows (Advanced Function → Download Boot with `loader/RK3288UbootLoader_V2.30.10.bin`); that leaves the tablet in Loader mode as well.
2. **The loader area** (idblock + `u-boot.itb`, raw eMMC sectors 0x0000..0x1FFF): `./write-loader-head.sh --board <id>`, or RKDevTool/AFPTool on Windows ("write by address 0") with `devices/<board>/head-4M.bin` — 4 MiB at eMMC sector 0.
3. **The parameter structures**: `./restore.sh --board <id> --parameters-only` (the loader-area parameter copy to tool LBA 0x0000, the p1 parameter partition to tool LBA 0x2000).
4. **The GSI images**: `./restore.sh --board <id>`.

Step 2 comes before step 3: with some tools the loader area and the loader-area parameter copy share an address range, and step 3 writes the parameter copy back.

### What `restore.sh` covers on its own

`restore.sh` alone covers **only** the damage class "bootloader intact, parameter structures erased". On such a tablet the bootloader is still on the eMMC, so the tablet is in Loader mode, every write of the restore is reachable, and the parameter structure can be read back from the tablet to detect the board.

On a blank tablet none of that holds:

- the tablet is in Maskrom mode, so `restore.sh` stops before it reads or writes anything — unless `--maskrom` is given, which pushes the loader into the RAM but does not write the loader area;
- **the board cannot be detected**: the auto-detection reads the parameter structure at tool LBA 0x2000 and compares it with the `parameter-p1.img` of every board in `devices/`. A tablet with no parameter structure has nothing to compare, so **`--board <id>` is required** on a blank tablet.

### `--maskrom` and the recorded `rkdeveloptool db` failure

`--maskrom` runs exactly one `rkdeveloptool db` with the bundled loader, waits about two seconds, probes the device again, and continues only when the tablet now reports `Loader`. If it does not, the tool's own output is printed and the script stops with exit code 5.

`rkdeveloptool db` **failed on this tablet three times on 2026-10-29**, and the failures are not a typo of this README:

| attempt | what the tool printed | what happened |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `The device does not support this operation!` | the tool judged the USB device to be in Loader mode and refused before it even opened the loader file (`strace`: the loader file was never opened) |
| 2 (the gate patched) | `Downloading bootloader failed!` and `DeviceRequest vendor=0x471 failed, err=-1` | request 0x0471 was sent and rejected |
| 3 | as above | the device fell off the USB bus (`usb 3-2.3: USB disconnect, device number 21`) and did not come back |

So the documented route for the Maskrom step is **RKDevTool/AFPTool on Windows**, not `rkdeveloptool db`. `restore.sh --maskrom` exists for the case where `db` works; it never runs `db` unless it is asked to.

### The raw sector map of the first 12 MiB

The first three regions of the eMMC are three contiguous 4 MiB blocks:

| raw eMMC sectors | MiB | what is there | tool LBA |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0x0000..0x1FFF | 0..4 | the loader area: idblock at sector 0x40, `u-boot.itb` (tag `RK32`) at sector 0x44 | below 0 — unreachable |
| 0x2000..0x3FFF | 4..8 | the loader-area parameter region: 8 copies of the parameter structure, 512 KiB apart | 0x0000..0x1FFF |
| 0x4000..0x5FFF | 8..12 | the p1 "uboot" partition: 4 copies of the parameter structure, 1 MiB apart | 0x2000..0x3FFF |

and the rule that connects the two columns:

```
tool LBA = raw eMMC sector - 0x2000
```

The loader area (raw 0x0000..0x1FFF) is therefore at tool LBA −0x2000..−1, below every tool LBA — which is why no `rkdeveloptool` LBA command can reach it. The rule was verified three ways on this tablet: the resource image sits at tool LBA 0x6000 = raw 0x8000, the kernel at tool LBA 0xE000 = raw 0x10000, the super partition at tool LBA 0x92000 = raw 0x94000, and the parameter structure the bootloader itself uses lists the same numbers.

### `write-loader-head.sh`

```sh
./write-loader-head.sh --board ifive-mini4s                   # the base is established from the tablet
./write-loader-head.sh --board ifive-mini4s --lba-base raw    # raw eMMC sector 0 is tool LBA 0x0000
./write-loader-head.sh --board ifive-mini4s --lba-base tool   # raw eMMC sector 0 is tool LBA 0x2000
```

The base of the LBA numbering is **established, never guessed**: the script reads the loader head back from the two candidate addresses (tool LBA 0x0000 and tool LBA 0x2000, both read from `devices/<board>/device.json`) and compares it with `head-4M.bin`. On a blank tablet both read as zeros, so the script refuses to write, says that the base cannot be detected and asks for `--lba-base`.

The script writes the loader area and nothing else: it writes `devices/<board>/head-4M.bin`, reads the region back, compares it byte for byte, checks the `RK32` tag at byte offset 0x8800, and never resets the tablet. It refuses to run when the tablet is not enumerated, when `devices/<board>/head-4M.bin` is missing, and it asks for the word `restore` before it writes.


## Device identity (build fingerprint)
The `build.prop` of both the system image and the vendor image in this package carries this
tablet's own identity instead of the GSI defaults:

```
ro.product.brand=iFive            ro.product.model=iFive mini 4S
ro.product.name=ifive_mini4s      ro.product.device=mini4s
ro.build.fingerprint=iFive/ifive_mini4s/mini4s:16/BP2A.250605.031.A3/250617:userdebug/test-keys
ro.build.flavor=ifive_mini4s-userdebug
```

Before this the image kept the GSI defaults (brand `google`, model `mainline`, device `generic`,
flavor `treble_a64_bvS`, and no `ro.build.fingerprint` key at all), which is why the About-phone
screen and apps such as browsers showed the tablet as an unnamed device.
`scripts/patch-identity.sh` writes those keys into both images at build time; the values can be
changed through `ID_BRAND` / `ID_MANUFACTURER` / `ID_MODEL` / `ID_NAME` / `ID_DEVICE`.
`ro.vendor.build.fingerprint` and `ro.bootimage.build.fingerprint` stay the ASUS Tinker Board
Android 11 strings, because those blobs really are that build.

## Optional: Magisk root (replacement boot partition)
Published next to this zip, not inside it: `ifive-mini4s-android16-boot-magisk-v31.img` with its
`.sha256` (Magisk 31.0, whose APK sha256 is `2c8a488b9a5293e578e95ae4f07e3c57aba4feec4a52ca4dd852a2692d6dd4e8`).
It replaces `images/ramdisk.img` at tool LBA `0x16000` — the same partition flash.sh writes:

```sh
sha256sum -c ifive-mini4s-android16-boot-magisk-v31.img.sha256
rkdeveloptool wl 0x16000 ifive-mini4s-android16-boot-magisk-v31.img
rkdeveloptool rd
```

Run `flash.sh` again to go back to the unrooted boot partition.

**The boot image alone is not a complete Magisk install on this tablet.** With only that partition
flashed, `magiskd` starts but stops in its post-fs-data stage every boot with
`* Magisk environment incomplete, abort` in `/cache/magisk.log`, and the app shows
`Ramdisk = Yes` together with `Not installed`. The app normally creates `/data/adb/magisk`
(busybox and the rest) as part of its own install, and it does that by unpacking and re-patching the
boot image - which it cannot do here, because this boot partition is a KRNL container
(`magiskboot unpack` rejects it).

A second thing gets in the way. The TrebleDroid GSI ships `/system/etc/init/su.rc`:

```
service sudaemon /system/bin/phh-su --daemon
    class main
```

That daemon mounts a tmpfs over Magisk's `/debug_ramdisk/magisk`, `/system/xbin/magisk` and
`/system/xbin/magiskpolicy`, with phh-su copied in place of each one (measured on the tablet: all
three were the same inode, sha256 of `/system/bin/phh-su`). So `/system/xbin/su` - Magisk's own
symlink `./magisk` - resolves to phh-su, which has no interface on this build: `me.phh.superuser` is
not installed and there is no `su.sqlite` anywhere in `/data`, so an app calling `su` is denied
without any prompt. And init's own Magisk boot-stage calls (`/debug_ramdisk/magisk --boot-complete`,
`--service`, `--zygote-restart`) run phh-su instead, which exits 2 on those options. Without
boot-complete the `bootloop` counter in `/data/adb/magisk.db` is never reset, and Magisk drops into
safe mode with every module off two boots later.

This image keeps phh-su: it belongs to the GSI and only matters once you use Magisk, so the
override is installed as part of the Magisk setup. After the tablet has booted once with the
patched image:

```sh
adb root
bash scripts/magisk-install-data.sh Magisk-v31.0.apk
adb reboot
```

That script does both halves. It fills `/data/adb/magisk` the way Magisk's own flashable-zip
installer does (`scripts/flash_script.sh`: rename `lib/<abi>/libX.so` to `X`, add `magisk32`, copy
`assets/`, drop `bootctl`/`main.jar`/`module_installer.sh`/`uninstaller.sh`, `chmod -R 755`), then
installs the module `ifive-no-phh-su` (built by `scripts/make-no-phh-su-module.sh`, published as a
release asset), which replaces `/system/etc/init/su.rc` and `/system/bin/phh-su` with empty files.
Everything it writes is in `/data`; no partition is touched.

Checked on the tablet after doing exactly that: `/cache/magisk.log` shows
`ifive-no-phh-su: loading module files` and then `** boot-complete triggered`, `bootloop` is `0`,
`su -c id` answers `uid=0(root) ... context=u:r:magisk:s0`, and `/debug_ramdisk/magisk` holds
Magisk's own binary again.

**"Requires additional setup" / "your device needs reflash Magisk".** That is Magisk's own
`env_check` (`assets/app_functions.sh:17-22`) returning 2: it wants the block node
`$MAGISKTMP/.magisk/device/preinit`, which magiskinit only creates when the ramdisk config carries
`PREINITDEVICE=<partition>`. Measured on the tablet with an image built without that key:
`/cache/magisk.log` keeps saying `mount: preinit dir not found` and `env_check` exits 2. The copy of
the same key in `/data/.backup/.magisk` does not help - `parse_config_file()` runs inside
`patch_ro_root()` / `patch_rw_root()` (`native/src/init/rootdir.cpp:264` and `:342`), long before
fs_mgr mounts `/data`. For this tablet the value is `mmcblk0p13`: `magisk --preinit-device` answers
that (`native/src/core/mount.rs:222` returns the basename of the mount source, and `/data` is a
candidate because `ro.crypto.state` is `unsupported` rather than `encrypted`), and magiskinit's
`find_block()` matches device names as well as partition labels
(`native/src/init/mount.cpp:85`). `scripts/make-magisk-boot.sh` writes the line into the config, and
`scripts/magisk-install-data.sh` prints the config it reads back from the boot partition, so you can
tell which image you are on.

Verified after writing that image: `env_check 31.0 31000` exits 0, `/debug_ramdisk/.magisk/device/preinit`
is a block device with major/minor `179, 13` (mmcblk0p13), and `/cache/magisk.log` now ends with
`* Found preinit dir: /data/adb` instead of the warning - the app stops showing the setup dialog.

The image is produced by `scripts/make-magisk-boot.sh` in the repository, from this package's
`images/ramdisk.img` plus a Magisk release APK. On this tablet the boot partition is a `KRNL`
container around a gzip cpio ramdisk (there is no `ANDROID!` boot image header), so
`magiskboot unpack`/`repack` cannot be used on it; the script instead runs the ramdisk operations
of Magisk's own `assets/boot_patch.sh` (`magiskboot cpio` with the same command list, `init`
replaced by the ARM 32-bit `magiskinit` matching the ramdisk's own `init`), gzips the result the
same way (`gzip -n -9`, verified to reproduce the stock bytes) and repacks the container with
`tools/mkkrnl.py`, which recomputes the CRC the bootloader checks.

`fstab.rk30board` is left byte-identical on purpose: it declares `fileencryption=software` on
`/data` and the vendor partition has no plain `/etc/fstab.rk30board` (only `.emmc`/`.sd`
variants), so Magisk's default stripping of those flags would change how `/data` mounts compared
with the image that boots today. `KEEPFORCEENCRYPT=false` selects Magisk's upstream behaviour.
Measured on the tablet running this package: `/data` is a plain ext4 on `/dev/block/mmcblk0p13`
with no device-mapper target behind it and `ro.crypto.state=unsupported`, so that declaration is
not in effect; not touching the file keeps the mount exactly as it works today.
The `magiskboot cpio` command list and the `.backup/.magisk` config written here follow Magisk
31.0's `assets/boot_patch.sh`; compared with 29.0 the list is the same and one line (`VENDORBOOT=false`)
was added to the config.
Checked offline on the finished file: `magiskboot cpio test` reports the ramdisk as Magisk patched,
`mkkrnl.py verify` reports the payload CRC, and unpacking the file gives the same members.
The tablet currently runs a Magisk boot image from this pipeline: seeded core `MAGISK_VER='31.0'
MAGISK_VER_CODE=31000`, `su -c id -Z` answers `u:r:magisk:s0`, and `/data/adb/modules` holds
`ifive-no-phh-su` and `ifive-tracefs-perms`. Whether that exact image came from the current
`scripts/make-magisk-boot.sh` (which derives `PREINITDEVICE` from the package's own partition table)
or from the earlier hand-set value is not recorded here; the derived value is `mmcblk0p13`, the same
one the script used to hard-code.

### One Magisk version on both sides

The patched boot image and the installed app have to come from the same Magisk release.
`env_check()` (Magisk's `assets/app_functions.sh:13-27`) compares the app's own version against
`MAGISK_VER` / `MAGISK_VER_CODE` in `/data/adb/magisk/util_functions.sh`; on a mismatch it returns
3 and the app shows "Requires additional setup" on every start. Rebooting does not clear it - only
making the two sides equal does. `scripts/magisk-install-data.sh` passes one APK argument to both
sides (step 4 installs the modules built from it, and it patches the boot image from the same file)
and its step 6 prints `seeded core` and `installed app versionCode` and names the fix on a mismatch.
Measured on the tablet after that procedure: `MAGISK_VER='31.0' MAGISK_VER_CODE=31000` and
`versionName=31.0 versionCode=31000` - equal, so `env_check 31.0 31000` exits 0 and the dialog stays
away.

Do not use the app's own "Direct install" / "In-place" on this tablet. That path hands
`/dev/block/by-name/boot` to `magiskboot unpack`, and this boot partition is a `KRNL` container with
no `ANDROID!` boot image header (see above), so `unpack` cannot get a ramdisk out of it; whatever
the app then writes is a raw boot image where the container belongs. The supported way is
`scripts/make-magisk-boot.sh` plus `rkdeveloptool wl 0x16000 <image>`.

## Charging policy
- Powered-off tablet on charger: `reboot -p` with the charger attached was tested; the panel stayed dark, USB charging was observed, and Android did not boot on its own.
- Charge-boost switch (`persist.vendor.chg.boost`) is verified on device: `vendor.chgpolicy` runs, `high_current_allowed=Y`, and the ADP input current limit rises to 2000000.

## Known issues
- Hard hang: rare, root cause unknown. Most earlier "hangs" were the host-side adb server wedging, not the tablet. The one hang with a kpanic ring behind it stopped at 86.7 s during init (netd/wificond start), with no kernel error.
- Hardware watchdog verified on the debug kernel: a hard hang resets in about 10–16 s and returns to maskrom via the loader flag. The release kernel runs with `rkwdt=0` — no timed reset and no watchdog, only the kpanic log dump.
- The native HWC2 port (drm_hwcomposer) does not initialise on this VOP (`init_success_=0`, plane 0 keeps the fbcon frame), so it was reverted; the release uses the patched HWC1 through the hwc2on1adapter.
- Every Chromium- or WebView-based app draws a black page (empty window, no text, no image). The GSI mounts tracefs at `/sys/kernel/tracing` with `gid=3012` (`init.rc:76`) and repairs its mode in `on late-fs` (`init.rc:678`), but on this tablet the directory stays `drwx------`, so when an app zygote forks a renderer it re-opens the inherited `trace_marker` descriptor, fails, and aborts: `JNI FatalError called: (zygote) Failed open(/sys/kernel/tracing/trace_marker, 1): Permission denied` (six identical SIGABRTs in `/data/tombstones/`, one per renderer start). This release carries the repair in `/vendor/etc/init/hw/init.rockchip.rc` (`chmod 0755 /sys/kernel/tracing` on the post-fs-data trigger, written by `scripts/patch-vendor.sh`); `scripts/make-tracefs-module.sh` builds the same repair as a Magisk module for tablets whose `/vendor` predates it. Check: `ls -ld /sys/kernel/tracing` → `drwxr-xr-x`.
- Pull-down of the notification shade is not smooth. Chasing it turned up two real bugs in this release's HWC, both fixed here: `MatchPlanes` rejected the whole frame whenever the bottom layer had a global alpha ("vop cann't support first layer with global alpha" — the 4.19 VOP actually exposes GLOBAL_ALPHA on every plane and blends against black exactly like GLES composition does), and the plane-overflow bandwidth cap was hardcoded to 3.3, a vendor figure from 1080p boards, which rejected this panel's 5-layer shade scene (~4.9) frame after frame. Before the fix every animated frame was GPU-composited by SurfaceFlinger; now it composes on VOP overlay planes (307-frame pull-down: 0 downgrades, 445 overlay commits, no dmesg warnings, pixels unchanged). The cap is tunable via `vendor.hwc.vop.max_scale` (default 6.0 on this board). Jank did not improve: 82-88% over interleaved runs, the same as the pre-fix 76-89%. A second round of analysis (evening ftrace, `reports/ShadeJankTrace1110.md`) located where the missing time actually goes, and found a third fixable bug: SurfaceFlinger spent a median 11.13 ms per frame GPU-rasterizing the wallpaper layer, because the HWC's mix policy widened its fallback window downwards first - always swallowing z=0, the full-screen wallpaper - while the small dynamic strips on top kept the overlay planes. This release's HWC (sha256 `7575aed5...`) widens the window upwards first, putting the wallpaper on a plane and only the thin strips on the GPU. What else was measured and is flat: GPU clock (`devfreq min_freq` 198->594 MHz and `governor=performance`), CPU clock — the cpufreq policy already runs all four cores at their 1.608 GHz cap mid-swipe, so there is no clock to win —, SF pipeline phase (`debug.sf.early/late.app.duration` 16.6 vs 33.2 ms), and `debug.sf.auto_latch_unsignaled`. The remaining cost is the render pipeline, measured per frame from the afternoon's ftrace capture: SystemUI's main thread burns only 8.0 ms of CPU per shade frame and then waits 18 ms for the GPU (`reports/ShadeJankTrace1110.md` has the breakdown and corrects this README's earlier 20-26 ms CPU figure); the shade is one full-screen 2048x1536 surface being rasterized at the board's 594 MHz GPU clock ceiling, which puts a ~27 ms floor under the frame time - 37 fps - for anything this package can configure. Rendering fewer pixels is the only lever that moves it consistently: `wm size 1152x1536` + `wm density 180` gave 76/79/80/82% against 87/88/87% native in the same session — about eight points, at visibly softer text; nothing in this package enables it, `wm size reset` + `wm density reset` undo it. Cutting the per-frame thread work would mean changing SystemUI, which ships inside the prebuilt TrebleDroid GSI; this repository builds the kernel, device tree, vendor partition and packaging. Full numbers and protocol: `reports/HwcFix1110.md` and `reports/Issues1110.md`.
  A tablet already flashed with the 2026-11-08 release does not need a reflash: install `ifive-hwc-composition.zip` (same release page) in the Magisk app or with `magisk --install-module`, reboot **twice** (the first boot only moves the module into place), and check with a root shell — `stat -c %s /vendor/lib/hw/hwcomposer.rk30board.so` must print `370484` (370240 means the old file is still live).
- "This site can't be reached" / `ERR_NAME_NOT_RESOLVED` in the browser, while other apps still use the network. This tablet was found with `Settings.Global.restricted_networking_mode = 1` — Android's restricted networking mode, which firewalls every uid that does not hold `CONNECTIVITY_USE_RESTRICTED_NETWORKS` (`dumpsys netpolicy` prints `Restricted networking mode: true` and `UID=<app> policy=262144 (REJECT_ALL)`, and the per-uid line reads `effective=RESTRICTED_MODE`). Under it the system reports no default network to those uids, so a browser that reads its resolver addresses from the system fails with `Failed to read DnsConfig` in logcat, while an app that resolves through bionic's resolver keeps working and the fault looks browser-specific. No script on the tablet writes that key (no match for the name in `/data/adb/service.d`, `/data/adb/post-fs-data.d`, `/data/adb/modules`, `/system/bin`, `/system/xbin`, `/system/etc`, `/system_ext/etc`, `/vendor/etc`, `/odm/etc`); what set it to 1 is unknown.
  Fix, from a host shell — nothing in the browser is touched:
  ```sh
  adb shell settings get global restricted_networking_mode     # 1
  adb shell settings put global restricted_networking_mode 0
  ```
  Verified on device: the per-uid `effective` loses `RESTRICTED_MODE`, `Failed to read DnsConfig` stops appearing, and `https://example.org` plus `https://github.com` draw full page content (screenshots taken on the tablet). The value is stored in `/data` and stays 0 across a reboot, so one command is enough.
